#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096346 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bf804d6b-ceba-558b-8cde-11d70906372f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3739" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8)$. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1046/Source/Images/8c0a30c74a9851f457e1a17295449e84.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0$, andthe tangent line exists at this point.\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2$. Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n- The point $x=4$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=4$, andthe tangent line exists at this point.\n- The point $x=6$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x = 6$, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the points of inflection are $x=0$ and $x=4$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096347 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "803d8981-7cc1-5f4a-bb47-21d7583e00ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-3742" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x$. The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1046/Source/Images/b6aea7e849502bdf3bd10c401a134e62.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x$.\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative $f''(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of $f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y=f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x = -4$ and $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-4$ and $x=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096348 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/id #uuid "ac3b585a-909a-5498-8c39-d8d03e378d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-3746" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection (if any) for $f(x) = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22$." :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0$. However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(x) & = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22 \\\\ f'(x) & = 3x^2 + 18x + 24 \\\\ f''(x) & = 6x + 18 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6x+ 18 &= 0 \\\\ 6x &= -18 \\\\ x &= -3. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$. So we pick two points around it, say, $x=-4$ and $x=-2$, and compute the sign of $f''(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(-4) &= 6(-4) + 18 =-6 < 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(-2) &= 6(-2) + 18 = 6 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3$, we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096349 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/id #uuid "ed7d94d2-25c5-53d9-830e-a659d304ecb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3747" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $f(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 + 6 x^2$." :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0$. However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{4} - 4x^{3} + 6x^{2} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 4x^{3} - 12x^{2} + 12x \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 12x^{2} - 24x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x^{2} - 24x + 12 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1$. So we pick two points around it, say,\n\n$\\qquad$ $x=0 \\quad$ and $\\quad x=2$,\n\nand compute the sign of $f''(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(0) &= 12(0)^{2} - 24(0) + 12 = 12 > 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(2) &= 12(2)^{2} - 24(2) + 12 = 12 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1$, we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a329ca-e000-513c-a624-fe32c9f29396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093760} {:answer/id #uuid "0774774a-5064-5ba7-8b6d-ab0d82cb1f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093761} {:answer/id #uuid "1f819151-c5cf-5b4d-8903-0cab4d515534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093762} {:answer/id #uuid "2141b62a-7e3d-59eb-9feb-9ef7a74970db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093763} {:answer/id #uuid "56b4071c-cfd6-5184-aa61-e04e7eeb99b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56b4071c-cfd6-5184-aa61-e04e7eeb99b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093764} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a4b055-23cf-5535-b288-ddb6c6e34d53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093759}] :question/id #uuid "c163d924-6190-52ae-9f3b-628d336cfc56" :question/math-academy-id "q-114407" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFind the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x) = e^{x}(x^{3} - 8x^{2} + 26x - 36)$." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065deb0c-5d57-546a-8c8d-c914f8b17320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095786} {:answer/id #uuid "2b03460f-477c-5b1d-b368-fe255ea85a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095787} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0c35f0-de90-5a67-88a5-46ccd8b6d36d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ and $x = 5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095788} {:answer/id #uuid "532d9d26-989b-5702-a201-7fe2e4b78002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095789} {:answer/id #uuid "54c1cb8b-f690-5b80-b7db-a62fed7b70bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c1cb8b-f690-5b80-b7db-a62fed7b70bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095790} :answer-field/id #uuid "19b35f28-2017-55f5-b4dc-61220e3e94f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095785}] :question/id #uuid "2792f399-d353-50d3-affd-f9260fbd9716" :question/math-academy-id "q-51428" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1046/Source/Images/q-51428.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y = f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?"}]}
